# ============================================================================
# TAU-NOMIC TUTORIAL 02 - DECIDING, NOT VOTING
# Why a decidable kernel has no use for majorities
# ============================================================================
#
# Part 1 showed THE capability: specifications are values, and questions
# about them are decidable arithmetic. This part shows the first consequence
# for governance - and it is a remarkable one:
#
#   A KERNEL THAT CAN DECIDE DOES NOT NEED TO COUNT.
#
# Voting exists because ordinary institutions cannot DERIVE what follows
# from their rules - so they aggregate opinions instead. Tau's algebra
# derives. And in a Boolean algebra, the meet is IDEMPOTENT:
#
#   r & r = r
#
# One law stated a thousand times is exactly one law. Read that as a
# governance statement: REPETITION HAS NO WEIGHT. There is no "more votes";
# there is only "consistent or not", "entailed or not".
#
# Run me:  tau -q < nomic_02_decide_not_vote.tau
# Verified against: Tau 0.7.0-alpha, build 240788e (alpha syntax may drift).
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T 0 T T F

set charvar off

# ============================================================================
# 1. FIVE "VOTES" ARE ONE LAW (idempotence)
# ============================================================================
# The same right R = "action o5 only with log o6", submitted five times.
# Is the five-fold meet exactly equal to R alone?

normalize (({ o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 } & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 } & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 } & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 } & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 }) = ({ o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 })).
# => T
#    A Sybil attack - one actor, many identities, same proposal - is not
#    "defended against" here. It is MEANINGLESS. The algebra cannot even
#    express the difference between one voice and a thousand.

# ============================================================================
# 2. REPETITION CANNOT OVERRIDE THE CORE
# ============================================================================
# The core says "never o1" ({ o1[t]=0 }). Three "votes" for the opposite:

normalize ({ o1[t]=0 } & { o1[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 } & { o1[t]=1 }).
# => 0
#    Still the impossible spec. A contradiction does not become law by
#    being demanded loudly. In a counting system, 3 beats 1. In a deciding
#    system, 0 stays 0.

# ============================================================================
# 3. WHAT IS ALREADY LAW CANNOT BE "PASSED AGAIN"
# ============================================================================
# Current law: core (o1 -> o4) plus the log right (o5 -> o6).
# Someone re-submits the log right. Does the legal situation change?

normalize (((({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 })) & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 }) = ({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 })).
# => T
#    Nothing changed - decidably. tau-nomic surfaces exactly this as its
#    verdict code 10, "already in force (idempotent)": the proposal is
#    acknowledged and the constitution is left untouched. No duplicate
#    entries, no inflation of the law by repetition.

# ============================================================================
# 4. CONSEQUENCES ARE NOT VOTED IN - THEY ALREADY HOLD
# ============================================================================
# Law: "o1 only with authorization o4" and "o4 only after review o5".
# Question: is "o1 only after review o5" - never explicitly enacted -
# already law?

normalize ((({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1 }) & { o1[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1 }) = ({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1 })).
# => T
#    The transitive consequence already HOLDS - without anyone proposing
#    it. A deciding kernel does not legislate consequences; they are
#    contained in the meet. (Ask a committee to notice every consequence
#    of its own bylaws. This is that, for free, with proof.)

# And a non-consequence stays out - no false positives:

normalize ((({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1 }) & { o1[t]=1 -> o7[t]=1 }) = ({ o1[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 } & { o4[t]=1 -> o5[t]=1 })).
# => F
#    o7 appears in no law, so "o1 -> o7" is NOT entailed. Deciding cuts
#    both ways: everything that follows, nothing that does not.

# ============================================================================
# 5. WHAT THIS MEANS - AND WHAT IT HONESTLY DOES NOT
# ============================================================================
#
# Inside the deciding kernel:
#   - majority is not a concept (idempotence),
#   - Sybil / vote-buying / concentration attack nothing (there is no vote),
#   - consequences of law are instantly, provably law.
#
# Honest boundary: voting does not disappear from the world - it retreats
# to its irreducible minimum. When two proposals are BOTH consistent with
# the core but conflict with each other, no algebra can pick one: that
# residual choice (and the deeper power of FRAMING what gets proposed at
# all) stays human. tau-nomic pushes voting to that edge and no further -
# part 7 of this series maps this boundary precisely.
#
# NEXT: nomic_03_living_constitution.tau - the kernel starts to RUN:
# a constitution as a temporal machine that accumulates its own law.
#
# ============================================================================
# END OF TUTORIAL 02
# ============================================================================
